\begin{equation}\label{equ2.12}
Ai(x,y)=
 \begin{cases}
 \dfrac{Ai(x)Ai'(y)-Ai(y)Ai'(x)}{x-y}, & x\ne y, \\
 Ai'(x)^2-xAi(x)^2 & x=y. \\
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Here $Ai(x)$ denotes the standard Airy function
\begin{align*}
Ai(x)=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_{\mathcal{C}}{e^{x\cdot t+t^3/3}\,\mathrm dt},
\end{align*}
where $\mathcal{C}$ is a contour running from $\infty e^{- i \pi /3}$ to $\infty e^{ i \pi /3}$, or any other contour which can be deform from this one such that $Re(t^3) \to -\infty$ along the contour.
Using the relation
\begin{align}\label{equ2.13}
Ai(x,y)=\int_0^{\infty}{Ai(t+x)Ai(t+y)\,\mathrm dt},
\end{align}
we have the following double integral representation of the Airy kernel
\begin{align*}
